notes.18-46:
LeetCode 46 — Permutations (Backtracking): The core idea is to build the permutation one position at a time using DFS. Maintain a `path` containing the current permutation and a `used[]` array to mark which elements have already been chosen. At each recursive call, iterate through all numbers, skip those already used, choose an unused number (`used = true`, `push_back`), explore recursively, and then undo the choice (`pop_back`, `used = false`) so the next branch starts with the original state. The base case is when `path.size() == nums.size()`, meaning a complete permutation has been formed and is added to the answer. The invariant to remember is: every recursive call must leave the state exactly as it found it.


Backtracking template:
if (path.size() == nums.size()) {
    ans.push_back(path);
    return;
}

for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
    if (used[i]) continue;

    used[i] = true;           // Choose
    path.push_back(nums[i]);

    backtrack(nums);          // Explore

    path.pop_back();          // Undo
    used[i] = false;
}

