notes.19-131:
### #131 — Palindrome Partitioning

Use **backtracking** because at every index `start`, we have multiple choices for where to end the next substring. We run `end` from `start` to `n-1`, so we try every possible substring `s[start...end]`. If that substring is a **palindrome**, we choose it by pushing it into `cur`, then recursively call `backtrack(end + 1)` because indices `start...end` have now been consumed and the next unprocessed character is `end+1`. Once that recursive path is completely explored, we **undo the choice** using `cur.pop_back()` and let the loop try a different `end`. When `start == n`, the entire string has been partitioned into palindromic pieces, so we add `cur` to `ans`. The core pattern is: **try every ending position → if valid, choose → recurse from `end+1` → undo**.

```cpp
for (int end = start; end < s.size(); end++) {
    if (isPalindrome(s, start, end)) {
        cur.push_back(s.substr(start, end - start + 1));
        backtrack(s, end + 1);
        cur.pop_back();
    }
}
```

**Mental model:** `start` = where I am currently, `end` = where I choose to cut the next piece.

notes.19-52:
## #52 — N-Queens II

Use **backtracking** to place exactly one queen in each row. For every row, try every column and place a queen only if that column and both diagonals are currently free. We track occupied columns using `cols`, and diagonals using `row - col` for the `\` diagonal and `row + col` for the `/` diagonal. After placing a queen, recurse to `row + 1`; after returning, remove it from all three sets to backtrack and try the next position. When `row == n`, all `n` queens have been placed successfully, so increment a `count`. Unlike **#51 N-Queens**, we don't need to store the board because the question only asks for the **number of valid arrangements**.

**Core pattern:**

```cpp
if (row == n) {
    count++;
    return;
}

for (int col = 0; col < n; col++) {
    if (cols.count(col) ||
        diag1.count(row - col) ||
        diag2.count(row + col))
        continue;

    cols.insert(col);
    diag1.insert(row - col);
    diag2.insert(row + col);

    backtrack(row + 1, n);

    cols.erase(col);
    diag1.erase(row - col);
    diag2.erase(row + col);
}
```

**Remember:**
`col` → column conflict
`row - col` → `\` diagonal
`row + col` → `/` diagonal
`row == n` → one valid solution → `count++`
